Notes on Building NH3.1.2 with Think C

In General

	The general idea is this.  The MPW version works so lets port as much of
the MPW code as we can, ifdef'ing as little as possible.

	Perhaps the most fundamental difference between the MPW environment and
the Think C environment is that ints are four bytes under MPW and two
bytes under a default Think C project.  This problem can be easily
remedied by modifiying the compiler settings of all Think C projects to
use four byte ints.  When this is done, we must also remember to
recompile any Think C library to use four byte ints (specifically ANSI
and unix libraries).

	Another difference appears to be the way in which the MPW standard io
libraries have implemented fread/fwrite (we say "appears" because the
difference is infered from the changes that had to be made under Think C
to get the code to work).  Under Think C, an fread call will translate \n
into \r.  This usually ok unless the data is binary in which case 13
becomes 10 (or is it 10 that becomes 13, we can never keep those darn
things straight).  What does this mean? It means that all files that are
binary must be opened as such.  Fortunately this has already been done
for other ports.  Just find the ifdef test that define RDMODE "rb" and
WRBMODE "w+b" and add a test for the Think C compiler to the ifdef.  (As
a note, the best way to test for compiling under Think C is to test for
THINK_C which will be defined as 5 under Think 5.0 and 6 under Think C
6.0).

	MPW and Think C also differ in the set of include files.  The standard
libraries, low mem, and other Macintosh support routines are organized
differently and, occasionally, defined inconsistantly.  What does this
mean to the Think C wanna be?  A bunch of casts here and there in the
source code, mostly in the mac port specific code (see below and the
patch code for specifics).

	MPW supports the pragma segment XXXX while Think C doesn't.  If you
don't care about your memory foot print, just ignore this difference.  If
you do care, figure out how to segment the code with out using a gross
use of including C code in C code and send us mail on how you did it.

	Think C does not have a shell, :-(.  MPW does.  Ugh.  This requires that
the two compilers, DgnComp and LevComp, be given the command line
arguments in some other way.  This is where we take advantage of some
other noble person's efforts.  In both compilers, there are test for
MAC_THINK5.  This test causes argv and argc to be set at run time to
compile all necessary files (no date checking, just brute force).  It
works, it was already there, so what the heck, just use it.

	The lack of shell also requires the combersome task of result file
management.  Our approach has been to move all result files into the top
most directory.  This is done so that NetHack can be compiled and run
from the top most directory.  (In the future, we want to modify the
compilers to place to result files into a result directory, and Nethack
to look for its files in the result directory).

	If that was enough, life with out a shell also requires the Nethack
resource file to be put
together by hand.  This is one area that Think C 6.0 has improved our
lives.  Under 6.0, we can
now add !.rsrc and !.r files as source to the project.  Dot rsrc files
will be added to the project rsrc file directly and dot r files will be
compiled and then added.  For those who are without Think 6.0, good luck.
 The best approach is to use the resource compiler to compile the dot r
file, name the result as the projects rsrc file.

	Makedefs is another story.  When run, a command window pops up.  Just
type in the options you require.  When the files are generated, some of
the result files will have to be moved to the top level directory (data
files) or to the include area (header files).

	Needless to say, all of the above stuff that we must do because of the
lack of shell also implies no Makefile which, so we must be aware of what
files that we have modified and which files are dependant upon this
modified file.  In other words, we must constantly ask ourselves "gee, do
we need to run Makedefs again?".  Life is tuff; this is to help you get
use to it.

	If you are like us, lazy, and just want to get the results of all of
this and what follows, you can find the actual source changes and think
projects at an ftp site to be announced soon.

	What follows are most of the steps that we took in compiling NH3.1.2. 
If some are left out, blame it on late night TV and a small PB screen. 
If you find something left out, or think a
little more detail is needed, and have a desire to be NH socially active,
send us some mail.

	This is the first release of these notes.  We are sure stuff has been
left out.  So if you hit a snag, feel free to contact us.  We want and
need to debug this port.

	We can be reached at brh@apple.com

The Steps

	-2.	Read the Think C manuals.  Know your compiler and your environment. 
They are your friends and you are stuck with them.
	
	-1.	Get Think Reference or buy a bunch of books that cover Macintosh
programming (the correct number of books can be determined when you start
seeing the same stuff over and over again -- or you run out of cash).
		
	0.	Learn C and the standard io library.
	
	From here, we will assume that you know how to build and compile
projects, how file names are resolved,  what a project's resource file is
for, how the resource compiler works, and how to compile a library.  If
you don't, repeat the above steps.
	
	1.	Grab the sources maintaining the well defined directory structure.
	
	2.	Build the project files: MakeDefs.!, DgnComp.!, LevComp.! and
NetHack.! in the top must directory.  All projects should have both far
code and far data set and the four byte int compiler setting set.  Set
the application size of NetHack.! to a few megs.  Set the application
size of LevComp to a meg (We suspect that there is a memory leak in there
somewhere).  All projects should contain the libraries MacTraps,
ANSI-long, and unix-long.  (Note: ANSI-long and unix-long are not
distributed with Think C.  They are just the standard libraries compiled
with the four byte int compiler setting set).
		
